se'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Drop BankCustomer\n# Explanation: Almost all values for this feature are 1 (has a bank account), so the column provides little to no variance for model learning.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Added Columns\n\n1. Income-to-Debt Ratio: Captures financial health.\n2. Employment Stability: Reflects job stability and work history relative to age.\n3. Credit Utilization: Incorporates debt, income, and default history to analyze financial behavior.\n\nThese engineered features directly relate to financial stability, creditworthiness, and likelihood of a successful credit card application, which should improve downstream model performance. Columns dropped are considered irrelevant or redundant to prevent noise in the dataset.", "Here are three proposed feature engineering steps to improve predictions for credit card application success, followed by explanations for each suggestion:\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Credit History Risk Score\n# Feature description: Combines PriorDefault and CreditScore into a single feature using the formula: CreditScore * (1 - PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Captures both historical credit risk (via PriorDefault) and current creditworthiness (via CreditScore). A higher combined value indicates better credit reliability despite past defaults.\ndf['Credit_History_Risk_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] * (1 - df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Debt-to-Age Ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of total debt to the individual's age.\n# Usefulness: Balances the concept of financial burden (debt) with life stage (age). Younger people with high debt may indicate high financial risk, while seniors with less debt might represent better credit stability.\ndf['Debt_to_Age_Ratio'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Added small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry Risk Score\n# Feature description: Performs target encoding on the 'Industry' column, assigning the mean success rate of credit card applications per industry as a new feature.\n# Usefulness: Different industries may have varying levels of financial stability and income potential, which can affect creditworthiness. Target encoding captures this information.\ndf['Industry_Risk_Score'] = df.groupby('Industry')['Answer'].transform('mean')  # 'Answer' is the target column\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Adult is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Adult column is redundant since we already have a detailed 'Age_Group' feature and corresponding one-hot encoded columns such as Age_Group_Middle_Aged and Age_Group_Senior.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Adult'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The BankCustomer column has low variability (most values are 1), making it less informative for prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Senior column can already be inferred from the 'Age_Group' feature and other one-hot encoded age group columns.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
