se'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Drop BankCustomer\n# Explanation: Almost all values for this feature are 1 (has a bank account), so the column provides little to no variance for model learning.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Added Columns\n\n1. Income-to-Debt Ratio: Captures financial health.\n2. Employment Stability: Reflects job stability and work history relative to age.\n3. Credit Utilization: Incorporates debt, income, and default history to analyze financial behavior.\n\nThese engineered features directly relate to financial stability, creditworthiness, and likelihood of a successful credit card application, which should improve downstream model performance. Columns dropped are considered irrelevant or redundant to prevent noise in the dataset.", "Here are three proposed feature engineering steps to improve predictions for credit card application success, followed by explanations for each suggestion:\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Credit History Risk Score\n# Feature description: Combines PriorDefault and CreditScore into a single feature using the formula: CreditScore * (1 - PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Captures both historical credit risk (via PriorDefault) and current creditworthiness (via CreditScore). A higher combined value indicates better credit reliability despite past defaults.\ndf['Credit_History_Risk_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] * (1 - df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Debt-to-Age Ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of total debt to the individual's age.\n# Usefulness: Balances the concept of financial burden (debt) with life stage (age). Younger people with high debt may indicate high financial risk, while seniors with less debt might represent better credit stability.\ndf['Debt_to_Age_Ratio'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Added small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry Risk Score\n# Feature description: Performs target encoding on the 'Industry' column, assigning the mean success rate of credit card applications per industry as a new feature.\n# Usefulness: Different industries may have varying levels of financial stability and income potential, which can affect creditworthiness. Target encoding captures this information.\ndf['Industry_Risk_Score'] = df.groupby('Industry')['Answer'].transform('mean')  # 'Answer' is the target column\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Adult is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Adult column is redundant since we already have a detailed 'Age_Group' feature and corresponding one-hot encoded columns such as Age_Group_Middle_Aged and Age_Group_Senior.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Adult'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The BankCustomer column has low variability (most values are 1), making it less informative for prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Senior column can already be inferred from the 'Age_Group' feature and other one-hot encoded age group columns.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature name: Default_Risk_Adjusted_Score\n# Feature description: This feature adjusts the CreditScore by penalizing it more when PriorDefault equals 1 using the formula: AdjustedScore = CreditScore * (1 - PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Captures the effect of prior defaults on an applicant's creditworthiness. If someone has PriorDefault = 1, this reduces their effective score, accounting for higher risk.\ndf['Default_Risk_Adjusted_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] * (1 - df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Debt_To_Age_Adjusted\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of Debt_Income_Ratio to Age, adjusted to give age context to debt levels.\n# Usefulness: Young individuals with high Debt_Income_Ratio are higher risk. This adds context to financial burden relative to life stage, highlighting applicants who may be stretched financially.\ndf['Debt_To_Age_Adjusted'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Prevents division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry_Target_Encoded_Risk\n# Feature description: Performs target encoding on the 'Industry' column by assigning the mean success rate of credit card approvals for each industry.\n# Usefulness: Different industries have different income stability and risk levels. Target encoding for Industry provides direct, data-driven insights into credit approval likelihood per job sector.\ndf['Industry_Target_Encoded_Risk'] = df.groupby('Industry')['Answer'].transform('mean')  # 'Answer' is the target column.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Senior column can already be inferred directly from the 'Age_Group' feature and existing one-hot encoded columns (e.g., Age_Group_Middle_Aged).\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Citizen is dropped\n# The Citizen column has a small number of distinct classes and is less likely to provide meaningful predictive power compared to other features like PriorDefault or Employment_Age_Ratio.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Industry_Risk_Level_Medium is dropped\n# The Industry_Risk_Level_Medium column overlaps with the new Industry_Target_Encoded_Risk feature, which provides a more detailed and data-driven representation of industry-specific risks.\ndf.drop(columns=['Industry_Risk_Level_Medium'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three proposed feature engineering steps and column drop suggestions to improve predictions for credit card application success:\n\n---\n\n### Proposed Feature Engineering Steps\n\npython\n# Feature name: Creditworthiness Ratio\n# Feature description: Combines CreditScore and Debt_Income_Ratio into a single metric using the formula: CreditScore / (Debt_Income_Ratio + 1e-5). \n# Usefulness: This measures how much creditworthiness (via CreditScore) is balanced against the financial burden (Debt_Income_Ratio). A higher ratio indicates better financial management and risk profile.\ndf['Creditworthiness_Ratio'] = df['CreditScore'] / (df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Age Risk Factor\n# Feature description: Creates a risk factor score by considering age and its squared term using the formula: (Age * PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Age typically correlates with financial responsibility, but coupling it with PriorDefault provides additional context. A higher product may indicate younger individuals with financial difficulties or mismanagement.\ndf['Age_Risk_Factor'] = df['Age'] * df['PriorDefault']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry Financial Stability Score\n# Feature description: Uses one-hot encoded columns for industry risk levels (Industry_Risk_Level_Low and Industry_Risk_Level_Medium) to compute a combined financial stability score: 2 * (Industry_Risk_Level_Low) + (Industry_Risk_Level_Medium).\n# Usefulness: Industries with low financial risk are given higher weight, while medium-risk industries receive moderate weight. This captures nuances in financial stability across professions.\ndf['Industry_Financial_Stability'] = 2 * df['Industry_Risk_Level_Low'] + df['Industry_Risk_Level_Medium']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant or Less Informative Features\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Middle Aged is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Middle Aged column can be inferred from other one-hot encoded Age_Group columns (e.g., Age_Group_Senior), making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Middle Aged'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# This column overlaps with other one-hot encoded Age_Group features and does not provide additional insights beyond Age data.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Citizen is dropped\n# The Citizen feature adds little predictive power as it is unrelated to the financial behaviors or metrics influencing credit card approval, based on domain knowledge.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n1. Creditworthiness Ratio: Highlights the balance of credit score and financial burden.\n2. Age Risk Factor: Incorporates age alongside historical default behavior for nuanced risk evaluation.\n3. Industry Financial Stability Score: Combines industry-level insights into a single stability indicator.\n\nRedundant categorical features (e.g., age group dummies) and irrelevant columns (like Citizen) were dropped to streamline the dataset for better model performance and interpretability."]###,
