###["### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nHere are the three proposed feature engineering steps to improve predictions for credit card application success:\n\n---\n\npython\n# Income-to-Debt Ratio\n# This feature calculates the ratio of income to debt, which gives insight into the financial stability of an individual.\n# Usefulness: A higher income-to-debt ratio suggests better financial health, which likely correlates with credit card eligibility. This helps the model better understand the relationship between income and debt.\ndf['income_debt_ratio'] = df['Income'] / (df['Debt'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Employment Stability: Years Employed / Age\n# This feature computes the proportion of years employed relative to the age of the individual.\n# Usefulness: A higher value indicates a longer work history compared to age, which could be a signal of financial responsibility and job stability—important factors for creditworthiness.\ndf['employment_stability'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / df['Age']\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Credit Utilization: Debt / (Income + Prior Default Adjustment)\n# This feature measures debt relative to income while accounting for prior defaults.\n# Usefulness: Low credit utilization (controlled by income and financial history) often indicates better repayment ability. Incorporating Prior Default adds more nuance to this calculation.\ndf['credit_utilization'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5 + df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Column Dropping (Optional, Based on Irrelevance or Redundancy)\n\npython\n# Drop ZipCode\n# Explanation: ZipCode is unlikely to provide meaningful information since it's treated as a numerical value without geographic context.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Drop DriversLicense\n# Explanation: Having a driver's license might not contribute strongly to financial behavior or creditworthiness.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Drop BankCustomer\n# Explanation: Almost all values for this feature are 1 (has a bank account), so the column provides little to no variance for model learning.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Added Columns\n\n1. Income-to-Debt Ratio: Captures financial health.\n2. Employment Stability: Reflects job stability and work history relative to age.\n3. Credit Utilization: Incorporates debt, income, and default history to analyze financial behavior.\n\nThese engineered features directly relate to financial stability, creditworthiness, and likelihood of a successful credit card application, which should improve downstream model performance. Columns dropped are considered irrelevant or redundant to prevent noise in the dataset.", "Here are three proposed feature engineering steps to improve predictions for credit card application success, followed by explanations for each suggestion:\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Credit History Risk Score\n# Feature description: Combines PriorDefault and CreditScore into a single feature using the formula: CreditScore * (1 - PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Captures both historical credit risk (via PriorDefault) and current creditworthiness (via CreditScore). A higher combined value indicates better credit reliability despite past defaults.\ndf['Credit_History_Risk_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] * (1 - df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Debt-to-Age Ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of total debt to the individual's age.\n# Usefulness: Balances the concept of financial burden (debt) with life stage (age). Younger people with high debt may indicate high financial risk, while seniors with less debt might represent better credit stability.\ndf['Debt_to_Age_Ratio'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Added small constant to prevent division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry Risk Score\n# Feature description: Performs target encoding on the 'Industry' column, assigning the mean success rate of credit card applications per industry as a new feature.\n# Usefulness: Different industries may have varying levels of financial stability and income potential, which can affect creditworthiness. Target encoding captures this information.\ndf['Industry_Risk_Score'] = df.groupby('Industry')['Answer'].transform('mean')  # 'Answer' is the target column\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Adult is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Adult column is redundant since we already have a detailed 'Age_Group' feature and corresponding one-hot encoded columns such as Age_Group_Middle_Aged and Age_Group_Senior.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Adult'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The BankCustomer column has low variability (most values are 1), making it less informative for prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Senior column can already be inferred from the 'Age_Group' feature and other one-hot encoded age group columns.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature name: Default_Risk_Adjusted_Score\n# Feature description: This feature adjusts the CreditScore by penalizing it more when PriorDefault equals 1 using the formula: AdjustedScore = CreditScore * (1 - PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Captures the effect of prior defaults on an applicant's creditworthiness. If someone has PriorDefault = 1, this reduces their effective score, accounting for higher risk.\ndf['Default_Risk_Adjusted_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] * (1 - df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Debt_To_Age_Adjusted\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of Debt_Income_Ratio to Age, adjusted to give age context to debt levels.\n# Usefulness: Young individuals with high Debt_Income_Ratio are higher risk. This adds context to financial burden relative to life stage, highlighting applicants who may be stretched financially.\ndf['Debt_To_Age_Adjusted'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Prevents division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry_Target_Encoded_Risk\n# Feature description: Performs target encoding on the 'Industry' column by assigning the mean success rate of credit card approvals for each industry.\n# Usefulness: Different industries have different income stability and risk levels. Target encoding for Industry provides direct, data-driven insights into credit approval likelihood per job sector.\ndf['Industry_Target_Encoded_Risk'] = df.groupby('Industry')['Answer'].transform('mean')  # 'Answer' is the target column.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Senior column can already be inferred directly from the 'Age_Group' feature and existing one-hot encoded columns (e.g., Age_Group_Middle_Aged).\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Citizen is dropped\n# The Citizen column has a small number of distinct classes and is less likely to provide meaningful predictive power compared to other features like PriorDefault or Employment_Age_Ratio.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Industry_Risk_Level_Medium is dropped\n# The Industry_Risk_Level_Medium column overlaps with the new Industry_Target_Encoded_Risk feature, which provides a more detailed and data-driven representation of industry-specific risks.\ndf.drop(columns=['Industry_Risk_Level_Medium'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three proposed feature engineering steps and column drop suggestions to improve predictions for credit card application success:\n\n---\n\n### Proposed Feature Engineering Steps\n\npython\n# Feature name: Creditworthiness Ratio\n# Feature description: Combines CreditScore and Debt_Income_Ratio into a single metric using the formula: CreditScore / (Debt_Income_Ratio + 1e-5). \n# Usefulness: This measures how much creditworthiness (via CreditScore) is balanced against the financial burden (Debt_Income_Ratio). A higher ratio indicates better financial management and risk profile.\ndf['Creditworthiness_Ratio'] = df['CreditScore'] / (df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Age Risk Factor\n# Feature description: Creates a risk factor score by considering age and its squared term using the formula: (Age * PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Age typically correlates with financial responsibility, but coupling it with PriorDefault provides additional context. A higher product may indicate younger individuals with financial difficulties or mismanagement.\ndf['Age_Risk_Factor'] = df['Age'] * df['PriorDefault']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Industry Financial Stability Score\n# Feature description: Uses one-hot encoded columns for industry risk levels (Industry_Risk_Level_Low and Industry_Risk_Level_Medium) to compute a combined financial stability score: 2 * (Industry_Risk_Level_Low) + (Industry_Risk_Level_Medium).\n# Usefulness: Industries with low financial risk are given higher weight, while medium-risk industries receive moderate weight. This captures nuances in financial stability across professions.\ndf['Industry_Financial_Stability'] = 2 * df['Industry_Risk_Level_Low'] + df['Industry_Risk_Level_Medium']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant or Less Informative Features\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Middle Aged is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Middle Aged column can be inferred from other one-hot encoded Age_Group columns (e.g., Age_Group_Senior), making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Middle Aged'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Senior is dropped\n# This column overlaps with other one-hot encoded Age_Group features and does not provide additional insights beyond Age data.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Senior'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Citizen is dropped\n# The Citizen feature adds little predictive power as it is unrelated to the financial behaviors or metrics influencing credit card approval, based on domain knowledge.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n1. Creditworthiness Ratio: Highlights the balance of credit score and financial burden.\n2. Age Risk Factor: Incorporates age alongside historical default behavior for nuanced risk evaluation.\n3. Industry Financial Stability Score: Combines industry-level insights into a single stability indicator.\n\nRedundant categorical features (e.g., age group dummies) and irrelevant columns (like Citizen) were dropped to streamline the dataset for better model performance and interpretability.", "### Step 1: Feature Engineering - Financial Responsibility Score\npython\n# Feature name: Financial_Responsibility_Score\n# Feature description: Combines CreditScore, PriorDefault, and Debt_Income_Ratio into an adjusted score using the formula: CreditScore / ((Debt_Income_Ratio + 1) * (1 + PriorDefault)).\n# Usefulness: By taking into account the credit score, financial burden, and history of defaults, this feature assesses the applicant's financial responsibility. A higher score indicates better financial behavior and lower risk.\ndf['Financial_Responsibility_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] / ((df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] + 1) * (1 + df['PriorDefault']))\nend\n\n### Step 2: Feature Engineering - Experience Normalized by Age\npython\n# Feature name: Experience_Normalized_Age\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the ratio of total employment years to the expected working years based on an individual's age.\n# Usefulness: It indicates how much of an individual's life has been dedicated to employment. A higher value suggests greater financial stability and work history, which are relevant to credit card qualification.\ndf['Experience_Normalized_Age'] = df['Employment_Age_Ratio'] / (1 - df['Age'] / 100)  # Assuming 100 as a rough cap for expected working age\nend\n\n### Step 3: Feature Engineering - Debt Burden Score\npython\n# Feature name: Debt_Burden_Score\n# Feature description: Measures the ratio of debt to age, adjusted with Debt_Income_Ratio using the formula: (Debt_Income_Ratio / Age).\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the burden of debt relative to the financial maturity (age) of the individual. Younger individuals with high debt ratios may indicate higher risk.\ndf['Debt_Burden_Score'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n### Dropping Columns\npython\n# Explanation why the column Citizen is dropped\n# The Citizen column is unrelated to financial metrics and provides no direct insight into creditworthiness or application success. It likely has low predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Middle Aged is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Middle Aged column can be inferred from other age-related features and one-hot encoded columns, making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Middle Aged'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Industry_Risk_Level_Low is dropped\n# The Industry_Risk_Level_Low column overlaps with other categorical industry features (e.g., Industry_Risk_Level_Medium). Consolidating redundant columns simplifies the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Industry_Risk_Level_Low'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Summary of New Features\n1. Financial_Responsibility_Score: Combines credit score, default history, and debt-to-income ratio into a single metric to assess financial responsibility.\n2. Experience_Normalized_Age: Normalizes employment experience by life stage, reflecting financial reliability.\n3. Debt_Burden_Score: Contextualizes debt relative to age, capturing the financial burden at different life stages.\n\nThese features are intuitive, balance numerical values with real-world financial reasoning, and minimize redundancy, improving prediction performance.", "Here are the proposed next steps for feature engineering and column dropping recommendations to improve credit card application success predictions:\n\n---\n\n### Proposed Feature Engineering Steps\n\npython\n# Feature name: Adjusted Credit Risk Score\n# Feature description: Combines CreditScore and PriorDefault dynamically using the formula: AdjustedScore = CreditScore / (1 + PriorDefault).\n# Usefulness: Penalizes applicants with prior defaults by reducing their effective credit score. This emphasizes credit risk derived from historical default behavior while retaining scaled contributions from CreditScore.\ndf['Adjusted_Credit_Risk_Score'] = df['CreditScore'] / (1 + df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Debt Burden Factor\n# Feature description: Creates a burden factor to evaluate the relationship between Debt_Income_Ratio and Age using the formula: DebtBurden = Debt_Income_Ratio * (1 / Age).\n# Usefulness: Highlights how debt burden scales with age by emphasizing financial pressure on younger applicants with high Debt_Income_Ratios, which may indicate higher risk.\ndf['Debt_Burden_Factor'] = df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] * (1 / df['Age'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Employment Consistency Score\n# Feature description: Combines Employment_Age_Ratio and Industry_Risk_Level_Low into a single feature: Score = Employment_Age_Ratio * (2 if Industry_Risk_Level_Low else 1).\n# Usefulness: Increases the weight of job stability for low-risk industries, as these professions are less volatile. This score balances personal employment history and job sector stability.\ndf['Employment_Consistency_Score'] = df['Employment_Age_Ratio'] * (2 if df['Industry_Risk_Level_Low'] else 1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant or Irrelevant Columns\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Middle Aged is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Middle Aged column overlaps with Age_Group_Senior, Age_Group_Adult, and Age_Group_Young indicators, making it redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Middle Aged'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Industry_Risk_Level_Medium is dropped\n# This column is redundant given the addition of Employment_Consistency_Score, which already captures nuanced industry risk insights dynamically.\ndf.drop(columns=['Industry_Risk_Level_Medium'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Citizen is dropped\n# The Citizen column provides minimal relevant information since citizenship type (ByBirth, ByOtherMeans, Temporary) is unlikely to strongly correlate with financial metrics or creditworthiness.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n1. Adjusted Credit Risk Score: Penalizes credit score dynamically based on prior defaults to reflect risk more accurately.\n2. Debt Burden Factor: Evaluates financial risk relative to age, emphasizing debt pressure on younger applicants.\n3. Employment Consistency Score: Combines job stability with industry risk for a nuanced feature tied to financial security.\n\nRedundant columns were dropped to streamline the dataset, reduce noise, and improve model interpretability. These changes holistically enhance the ability to predict successful credit card applications while remaining intuitive and grounded in real-world rationale."]###,
