2026-05-23 20:19:46,960 - INFO - Selected best state: ("python\n# New Feature: Age_Group\n# Feature description: Categorizes individuals into age groups such as young adult (18-25), adult (26-40), middle-aged (41-60), senior (60+).\n# Usefulness: Age often impacts creditworthiness and credit card application approval; younger individuals may be less likely to qualify due to lower employment history or income stability, while middle-aged individuals typically have greater earning potential. Categorization can help a model better capture age-related trends.\ndf['Age_Group'] = pd.cut(\n    df['Age'], \n    bins=[15.75, 25, 40, 60, 76.75], \n    labels=['Young Adult', 'Adult', 'Middle Aged', 'Senior'], \n    include_lowest=True\n)\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['Age_Group'], drop_first=True)\nend\n\npython\n# New Feature: Debt-to-Income Ratio (DTI)\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of debt to income as a new feature: Debt / (Income + 1). A small offset (+1) is added to avoid division errors when income is zero.\n# Usefulness: This ratio is a major determinant in credit card approvals. High debt combined with low income would indicate an individual may be a risky borrower, whereas low DTI is often associated with better creditworthiness.\ndf['Debt_Income_Ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# New Feature: Employment Stability Index\n# Feature description: Combines YearsEmployed and Employed status into a single feature using the formula: Index = YearsEmployed * Employed. For individuals employed (Employed=1), this feature reflects tenure; for those unemployed (Employed=0), it’s simply zero.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the combined impact of job stability and employment status. Long tenures are positively correlated with financial stability and increase the likelihood of credit card approval.\ndf['Employment_Stability_Index'] = df['YearsEmployed'] * df['Employed']\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping Column: ZipCode\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped: \n# ZipCode is a numerical feature but does not provide meaningful, generalizable information for credit card predictions. It is likely a proxy for geographic location, which is not directly relevant unless specific regional trends exist in the dataset (which there is no evidence of here).\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nHere are the three proposed feature engineering steps to improve predictions for credit card application success:\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature: Prior Default Impact Score\n# Feature description: Combines PriorDefault, CreditScore, and Debt_Income_Ratio into a single feature to evaluate credit risk using the formula: Score = (1 - PriorDefault) * CreditScore / (Debt_Income_Ratio + 1e-5).\n# Usefulness: This feature reflects the combined impact of no prior defaults, creditworthiness, and financial responsibility. A higher score signals better credit risk.\ndf['Prior_Default_Impact_Score'] = (1 - df['PriorDefault']) * df['CreditScore'] / (df['Debt_Income_Ratio'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature: Employment to Age Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of years employed to current age using the formula: Ratio = YearsEmployed / Age.\n# Usefulness: A higher ratio indicates an individual's work experience relative to their age, which can signal financial stability and responsibility.\ndf['Employment_Age_Ratio'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / df['Age']\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature: Industry Risk Level\n# Feature description: Categorizes industries into low, medium, and high-risk levels based on general job security and income stability (e.g., Financials = low risk, Hospitality = high risk).\n# Usefulness: Job sectors play a role in creditworthiness due to differences in average income and job stability. Adding this feature provides industry-level insight into risk.\nindustry_risk_map = {\n    'CommunicationServices': 'Medium',\n    'ConsumerDiscretionary': 'High',\n    'Energy': 'Medium',\n    'InformationTechnology': 'Low',\n    'Education': 'Medium',\n    'Financials': 'Low',\n    'Healthcare': 'Low',\n    'Materials': 'Medium',\n    'ConsumerStaples': 'Low',\n    'Real Estate': 'Medium',\n    'Industrials': 'High',\n    'Research': 'Medium',\n    'Utilities': 'Low',\n    'Transport': 'High',\n}\ndf['Industry_Risk_Level'] = df['Industry'].map(industry_risk_map)\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['Industry_Risk_Level'], drop_first=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The majority of values in the BankCustomer column are 1 (has a bank account), so this column provides minimal variance for the model and is likely redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Age_Group_Adult is dropped\n# The Age_Group_Adult column is redundant because it overlaps with other categorical age group indicators like Age_Group_Middle Aged and Age_Group_Senior.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age_Group_Adult'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Added Features\n\n1. Prior Default Impact Score: Combines multiple critical factors into a single feature representing financial risk and creditworthiness.\n2. Employment to Age Ratio: Highlights job stability relative to age, improving insights into financial reliability.\n3. Industry Risk Level: Encodes job sector categories into risk levels to capture industry-specific credit risk trends.\n\nThese features are designed to better utilize both numerical and categorical data to improve downstream model performance while minimizing redundancy."), with improvements -
